# Kira language surface

Facts that trip up new writers.

## Syntax facts

- Declarations initialize with braces: `name: Type { value }`, a struct `name: Pt { x = 1, y = 2 }` or `{ 1, 2 }`
  (a lone value is the value itself); `=` only names a field or an argument, or changes a `Mutable`.
  The old `name: Type = expr` is refused; `kira --fix-decl-equals FILE...` rewrites it. A value of function type
  is written `f: Fx<T1<Int32>, Int32> { g }`.
- Ranges: `a..b` holds a, b and every number between (a `Range<T>` value too); `until`, `indices()` and the `Until` type are
  removed (the diagnostics show the `a..b` form). A loop is `while`; `kira --fix-for [--typed] FILE...` rewrites a `for`
  (`--typed` asks the typer, in the project above the file, for an iterable the file does not write).
- `with` scopes calls and assignments on one value: `x: Clazz { ... } with { f(...)  field = v }` or `x with { ... }`, never
  `with x { }`. A bare `x with { }` statement on a value type is an error (assign it, `x = x with { }`, or make the type a Mutable
  class, whose `with` edits in place).
  - The receiver: a class of the project, or a Mutable stdlib type (`List`, `Map`, `Set`, `Stack`, `Queue`, `Deque`, `Json`,
    `StrBuf` and the other `Mutable<..>` ones), edited in place: `j with { put("a", 1)  put("b", 2) }` is `j.put("a", 1)` then
    `j.put("b", 2)`, on the same Json. A stdlib value type (`Str`, a number, `Arr`, `View`, `Maybe`) is refused: nothing
    changes one in place. A Struct's `with` builds an edited copy, so it has a value to use and is never a statement.
  - The receiver is evaluated once, before the block; the value of the `with` is the receiver. A local, a parameter or a
    field that is no Mutable binding is used by its own name; a declaration's own `x: T { v } with { ... }` builds in `x`;
    any other receiver (a call, a Mutable field) is held in a temporary for the block.
  - Bare names in the block: a bare call `f(...)` is the receiver's method `f` when it has one, before the enclosing
    class's and the module's. A bare name that is not called is the receiver's field when it has one; a stdlib receiver
    has no reachable field, and a method is never a value, so a name that is only a method of the receiver (`size`, `keys`)
    means what it means outside the block: `xs with { add(size) }` adds the local `size`.
  - A local or parameter that shares the name is never guessed: a call `f(...)` with a local or parameter `f` in reach,
    or a name `n` that is both a local or parameter and a field of the receiver, is `types.with.clash`. The fix is in the
    message: name the member through the receiver (`xs.size()`, `c.frames`), or rename the local. A local declared in the
    block cannot be named like a field of the receiver. A block inside another whose receivers share a member name is
    `types.with.nested`: build the inner value in a local first.
  - `rules.with.fold` hints a run of statements in a row on one Mutable class value or one stdlib container or builder
    (`List`, `Map`, `Set`, `Stack`, `Queue`, `Deque`, `Json`, `StrBuf`, `ArgSpec`); `kira --fix-with [--dry-run]
    [--at-declaration] FILE-OR-DIR...` folds them, and with `--at-declaration` puts a run right under the declaration of
    `x` onto it (`x: T { v } with { ... }`) and joins a `x with { }` block already there. It folds only what keeps its
    meaning and lists what it leaves with why: a value type, a local or parameter named like the member used, a bare
    name the block would read as the receiver's member, a Mutable field or global a call in the statement could rebind.
    Py and cpp projects only: C and JS do not lower a `with`; C++ lowers one that edits in place (a stdlib value by
    reference, never a copy) and refuses a value type's.
- A statement ends at its newline: `;` is refused; `kira --fix-semicolons FILE...` drops them (never one in a string or a comment).
- Class fields: `require pub name: Type` (constructor parameter + public field).
  Construction is positional: `Point { 0, 1 }`, nested `Rect { Point { 0, 1 }, Point { 1, 0 } }`.
- Methods: `pub fx name: (args) ReturnType { ... }`. The C backend lowers
  methods to `Type_method(Type* this, ...)`.
- Modules: `module "app:main"` maps to `src/app/main.kira`. Multi-file use:
  `use "app:model"`.
- Stdlib modules are `kira:*` (`kira:stl` indexes core/tuples/collections/
  result/io/math). Magic types are ambient once the stdlib dependency loads;
  you do not write `use "kira:stl"` in app code.
- `trace(...)` prints with a newline; `print` / `println` / `eprint` / `assert`
  come from `kira:io`.

## Backend truth

- `if/else` and `while` (the only loop; `for` is refused, `kira --fix-for` rewrites it) lower today. Variants and generic
  traits are not lowered; trait inheritance and dispatch are.
- Classes are heap ARC (scope-end release). Field-embedded objects are
  borrowed; release on explicit return paths is skipped (leak, not crash).
- Container elements erase to a 64-bit `KiraSlot`: `Float32`/`Float64`
  elements are rejected, not silently reinterpreted.
- `Map.get`, `Stack.pop`, `Queue.dequeue` return `Maybe<T>`; payloads cast
  back at use sites.
- `@_opaque` / `@_extern` are the foreign edge: C symbols as written, never
  ARC'd. `build.cSources` / `build.linkFlags` carry extra C.
